CAT = "sgsecCrime"; SEG = "CRM"
# Crime, detention and conflict: PLRD refresher training package (Incident Response, Basic Conflict
# Management Skills, protection for officers), Conduct Regulations 2009 regs 7 and 7A, PSIA s 17A,
# SCDF Civil Defence Emergency Handbook (unruly crowds, useful numbers). No fines or penalty figures.
Q = [
("Why must an officer keep watch over a suspect they have detained?",
 "To stop escape or loss of evidence", ["To question him for the report", "To search him for stolen items", "To take down his statement"],
 "The PLRD package gives three reasons: to prevent escape, to prevent the suspect destroying evidence, and to prevent them injuring themselves or others."),
("A detained suspect starts banging his head on the wall. Why is this the officer's concern?",
 "He must be kept from injury", ["It proves that he is guilty", "It shortens his detention", "It is the client's problem"],
 "Guarding a detained suspect includes preventing them from causing injuries to themselves or others until the police take over."),
("Which of these is listed by PLRD as a criminal offence an officer may meet at work?",
 "Outraging modesty", ["Breach of a tenancy", "Breach of contract", "Trespass to goods"],
 "PLRD's examples of criminal offences include criminal trespass, outraging modesty, theft, possession of a weapon, vandalism and substance abuse."),
("A man enters a fenced compound at night to steal tools. Besides theft, which offence fits?",
 "Criminal trespass", ["Unlawful assembly", "Criminal breach of trust", "Rioting in public"],
 "Entering property with intent to commit an offence is criminal trespass, which PLRD lists among the offences officers commonly encounter."),
("When police arrive for a suspected theft, what should the officer hand over?",
 "The suspect and the item", ["The item and the report", "The suspect and the CCTV", "The report and the CCTV"],
 "In the PLRD scenario, the officer hands over the suspect and the 'stolen' item to the police, then updates the Control Room and records the incident."),
("While waiting for police, where should a seized item from a suspected theft be kept?",
 "Somewhere secured", ["On the open counter", "With the suspect", "At the officer's home"],
 "The PLRD scenario says to keep the item in a secured place until it is handed to the police."),
("Why should an officer spot signs of disorderly or threatening behaviour early?",
 "To act before it escalates", ["To record it for the police", "To identify the person fast", "To warn the other officers"],
 "PLRD's conflict guidance says recognising the signs lets officers pre-empt and take proactive action to prevent disorderly and threatening behaviour."),
("Besides alcohol, drugs and mental illness, what other disorderly behaviour does PLRD's conflict guidance list?",
 "Behaviour likely to breach the peace", ["Behaviour likely to delay visitors", "Behaviour likely to annoy tenants", "Behaviour likely to block the lifts"],
 "PLRD's examples of disorderly and threatening behaviour are behaviour under the influence of alcohol or drugs, behaviour due to mental illness, and behaviour that can cause a breach of the peace."),
("Why should difficult situations be handled promptly and with discretion?",
 "To calm the upset parties", ["To get the facts recorded", "To protect the site's image", "To free up the post sooner"],
 "Dealing with difficult situations promptly, efficiently and discreetly prevents escalation and cools upset or enraged parties."),
("Which technique does PLRD recommend when dealing with an angry visitor?",
 "Active listening", ["Raising your voice", "Turning away calmly", "Repeating the rules"],
 "PLRD advises staying calm and patient, approaching professionally, listening actively, and being polite, empathetic and firm."),
("Which phrase should an officer avoid because it angers people?",
 "\"You are wrong\"", ["\"Please calm down now\"", "\"Let me check for you\"", "\"That is the site rule\""],
 "PLRD lists phrases to avoid, including \"Listen to what I say\", \"You are wrong\", \"You did not answer my question\" and \"You do what I said\"."),
("Why does PLRD say to treat people under the influence of alcohol differently from other disorderly people?",
 "They may lose control", ["They may not remember it", "They may claim injury later", "They may become drowsy"],
 "People under the influence of alcohol or drugs can get emotional and lose control, so officers deal with them differently from other disorderly groups."),
("A dispute escalates beyond the officer's control. What does PLRD advise?",
 "Get the supervisor or police in", ["Record it all on paper first", "Separate the parties yourself", "Ask bystanders to step in"],
 "If an incident escalates beyond control, PLRD advises calling the supervisor and/or the police to intervene."),
("An off-duty officer swears at a neighbour at home. Under Conduct Regulation 7A, this is:",
 "Not covered by regulation 7A", ["An offence under regulation 7A", "An offence only if in uniform", "An offence for his agency, not him"],
 "Regulation 7A(1) applies when a licensed officer is carrying out the functions of a security officer; it then bans threatening or abusive language. Off duty at home, the officer is not carrying out those functions, so 7A does not apply (other laws still may)."),
("A crowd outside the building is turning unruly. What should the officer do?",
 "Move away and dial 999", ["Stay near and dial 995", "Approach and ask them to leave", "Lock the lobby and wait"],
 "SCDF's handbook says to stay calm, move away, not approach or confront the crowd, and dial 999 to report it to the Police."),
("When reporting an unruly crowd to the police, which detail is most useful?",
 "Whether any carry weapons", ["Names of the crowd leaders", "Which group started it", "What the crowd is chanting"],
 "SCDF asks callers to give the crowd's size, activity, location and further description such as mood and whether anyone carries weapons."),
("An officer is hurt while trying to stop a fight. Which number brings SCDF medical help?",
 "995", ["999", "1777", "70999"],
 "995 is SCDF's fire and emergency ambulance number. 999 is the Police, 1777 is the non-emergency ambulance, and 70999 is the Police SMS line."),
("A shopper insults and threatens an officer who is enforcing a no-smoking rule. Where can the officer report such abuse at work?",
 "The USE Mobile app", ["The SGSecure app", "The myResponder app", "The MOM WorkRight portal"],
 "PLRD points officers facing abuse or work grievances to the Union of Security Employees' USE Mobile app, or the Security Industry Council's email helpline."),
("An officer leaves the site for an hour to run a personal errand. What does this breach under regulation 7A?",
 "The ban on absence without reason", ["The rule against sleeping on duty", "The duty to respond to requests", "The ban on false representation"],
 "Regulation 7A(1)(c) says an officer carrying out security functions must not be absent from the place of deployment without a valid reason. A personal errand is not one."),
("While guarding a detained suspect, which action by the officer is correct?",
 "Watching him until police come", ["Searching his bag for evidence", "Taking his written statement", "Cuffing him to a railing"],
 "The officer guards the suspect to prevent escape, destruction of evidence and injury, and hands over to the police. Questioning and searching phones are police work."),
("Section 17A of the PSIA covers criminal force used on an officer in which situation?",
 "During the officer's duty", ["Only when off duty", "Only with a weapon", "Only on state land"],
 "Section 17A covers assault or criminal force on an officer in the execution of duty, to deter them from duty, or because of something done in the lawful discharge of duty."),
("A man is found sniffing glue in the stairwell. Which offence on PLRD's list does this fit?",
 "Substance abuse", ["Vandalism", "Outraging modesty", "Criminal trespass"],
 "Abusing glue or drugs fits substance abuse, which PLRD lists among the criminal offences officers may meet. The officer keeps a safe distance, reports it and calls for help."),
("A teenager sprays graffiti on the lobby wall. Which offence on PLRD's list does this fit?",
 "Vandalism", ["Theft", "Unlawful assembly", "Outraging modesty"],
 "Damaging property with paint is vandalism, one of the criminal offences PLRD lists alongside theft, trespass and possession of a weapon."),
("A man in the car park shows a knife to scare passers-by. Which offence on PLRD's list fits?",
 "Possession of a weapon", ["Outraging modesty", "Vandalism of property", "Substance abuse"],
 "Carrying a knife to threaten people fits possession of a weapon. The officer keeps people away, keeps distance and calls 999."),
]
